    'body' => <<<'HTML'
<div class="key-takeaways"><h2>Key takeaways</h2><ul>
<li>Texas Insurance Code Chapter 542, Subchapter B sets deadlines for insurers to acknowledge, investigate, decide, and pay most property claims.</li>
<li>The deadlines can be extended by 15 days after a declared weather catastrophe, and the decision clock does not start until the insurer has the information it reasonably requested.</li>
<li>Most stalled claims come down to a handful of causes: adjuster turnover, repeat inspections, rolling document requests, engineer reports, and mortgage-company check handling.</li>
<li>A written timeline, complete responses, and dated follow-up letters are the most practical tools for moving a claim forward. A TDI complaint and an attorney referral are the next steps when those do not work.</li>
</ul></div>

<p>Few things wear on a property owner like waiting. The roof is tarped, the drywall is cut out, and the insurance company has gone quiet. If you are wondering why property insurance claims are delayed in Texas, the short answer is that the law sets clear deadlines, but several common situations can stretch or restart them. This guide explains the Texas Prompt Payment Act timelines, the most common reasons a claim stalls, and the practical steps homeowners and business owners in San Marcos, Hays County, and nearby Central Texas communities can take to get a decision.</p>

<p>We write this as a <a href="/">San Marcos public adjuster</a> that handles delayed, underpaid, and disputed claims. Nothing here is legal advice. For questions about lawsuits, bad faith, or deadlines to sue, talk with a licensed Texas attorney.</p>

<h2>What the Texas Prompt Payment Act requires</h2>

<p>The rules most people call the "Prompt Payment Act" are found in <a href="https://statutes.capitol.texas.gov/Docs/IN/htm/IN.542.htm" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Texas Insurance Code Chapter 542, Subchapter B</a>, titled Prompt Payment of Claims. They apply to most first-party property claims, including homeowners, landlord, and commercial property policies. They set a sequence of deadlines, each triggered by a different event.</p>

<table>
<thead><tr><th>Stage</th><th>Deadline for the insurer</th><th>What starts the clock</th></tr></thead>
<tbody>
<tr><td>Acknowledge the claim, begin the investigation, and request needed information (§542.055)</td><td>15 days</td><td>Receipt of notice of the claim</td></tr>
<tr><td>Accept or reject the claim in writing (§542.056)</td><td>15 business days</td><td>Receipt of all items, statements, and forms the insurer reasonably requested</td></tr>
<tr><td>Extension if the insurer needs more time to decide (§542.056)</td><td>Up to 45 additional days</td><td>The insurer notifies you it needs more time and explains why</td></tr>
<tr><td>Pay the claim after accepting it (§542.057)</td><td>5 business days</td><td>Notice that the claim (or part of it) is accepted</td></tr>
<tr><td>Weather-related catastrophe or major natural disaster declared by the commissioner (§542.059)</td><td>Each deadline above extended by 15 days</td><td>The declaration</td></tr>
</tbody>
</table>

<p>Two details matter more than most people realize. First, the decision clock starts when the insurer receives everything it <em>reasonably requested</em>, not when you first reported the loss. Second, the rules are not identical for every company. Eligible surplus lines insurers, which write some commercial and hard-to-place property, operate under different timeframes (30 business days for several of these steps). Your policy and your insurer's licensing status determine which timeline applies.</p>

<p>Chapter 542 also has consequences for insurers that do not pay covered claims on time. How those apply to a particular claim is a legal question, so it belongs with an attorney rather than an adjuster.</p>

<h3>The 15-day catastrophe extension</h3>

<p>After a large hail or windstorm, thousands of claims can land on the same desks at once. Texas law recognizes that by extending the Prompt Payment deadlines by 15 days when the claim arises from a weather-related catastrophe or major natural disaster, as defined in the statute. Hays County has seen that kind of event. On May 9, 2024, the NWS recorded 3-inch hail at San Marcos Academy west of the city, 2.5-inch hail near the outlet mall, and 70 mph wind gusts that damaged roofs and downed trees (<a href="https://www.ncdc.noaa.gov/stormevents/eventdetails.jsp?id=1181388" target="_blank" rel="noopener">NOAA NCEI event record</a>). Storms like that drive claim volume up across the I-35 corridor, and a slower first response is common. An extension adds time. It does not erase the deadlines.</p>

<h2>Common reasons property insurance claims are delayed</h2>

<p>In our experience, delays rarely come from one dramatic problem. They come from ordinary friction that piles up. These are the causes we see most often on claims in San Marcos, Kyle, Buda, Wimberley, and the surrounding area.</p>

<h3>Adjuster turnover and reassignment</h3>

<p>After a big storm, insurers bring in independent and catastrophe adjusters from out of state. Those adjusters may rotate off the storm after a few weeks, and your file moves to someone new. The new adjuster may need to review photos, re-read notes, and sometimes start the inspection discussion over. Each handoff can cost days or weeks. Asking for the name, direct contact, and supervisor of each new adjuster in writing helps keep the file from going dark.</p>

<h3>Re-inspections</h3>

<p>A second inspection is sometimes legitimate, for example when new damage is found during tear-off or when the first adjuster could not safely access a steep roof. It can also become a stall when one inspection follows another with no decision in between. Request a written reason for each re-inspection and the date the insurer expects to decide afterward.</p>

<h3>Rolling document requests</h3>

<p>Because the decision clock depends on the insurer receiving what it reasonably requested, a series of small, separate requests can keep resetting the timeline. Common requests include repair estimates, photos, receipts, contents lists, prior repair records, a signed proof of loss, and recorded statements. Answer every request completely, in writing, and keep proof of the date you sent it. If a request seems unrelated to the loss, ask the adjuster to explain how it relates to the claim.</p>

<h3>Engineer and specialist reports</h3>

<p>On foundation, wind, hail, and water claims, insurers often hire an engineer to give an opinion on cause of loss. Scheduling the visit, waiting for the report, and then waiting for the adjuster to act on it can add weeks. You can ask for a copy of any report the decision relies on. If you disagree with it, your own documentation and, where appropriate, an independent opinion may help answer it.</p>

<h3>Mortgage-company checks</h3>

<p>Even after the insurer pays, money can stall. Dwelling payments are often made out to both the owner and the mortgage company. Many lenders deposit the funds into a loss-draft account and release them in stages after inspections or paperwork. That process belongs to the lender, not the insurer, so ask your mortgage servicer early for its written loss-draft requirements.</p>

<h3>Disagreement over scope or price</h3>

<p>Sometimes the claim is not delayed so much as stuck. The insurer has paid what it thinks is owed, the contractor says the estimate will not cover the work, and nobody is moving. That is a disagreement over the amount, and it calls for a different set of tools, such as a detailed estimate comparison or a <a href="/services/insurance-claim-supplements/">supplement</a>, rather than more waiting.</p>

<table>
<thead><tr><th>Delay cause</th><th>What it usually looks like</th><th>A practical response</th></tr></thead>
<tbody>
<tr><td>Adjuster turnover</td><td>New adjuster, repeated questions, slow replies</td><td>Request the new adjuster's and supervisor's contact details in writing; send a one-page claim summary</td></tr>
<tr><td>Re-inspection</td><td>Another visit scheduled, no decision</td><td>Ask for the reason and a target decision date in writing; attend with your documentation</td></tr>
<tr><td>Document requests</td><td>Repeated requests for more items</td><td>Respond completely, keep dated proof, ask how each item relates to the loss</td></tr>
<tr><td>Engineer report</td><td>Waiting on a third-party opinion</td><td>Ask for the report date and a copy of any report relied on</td></tr>
<tr><td>Mortgage-company check</td><td>Payment issued but funds held</td><td>Get the lender's loss-draft requirements and inspection schedule in writing</td></tr>
<tr><td>Scope or price dispute</td><td>Payment made, repairs cannot be completed for that amount</td><td>Compare estimates line by line; request a supplement or reinspection</td></tr>
</tbody>
</table>

