<h2>How to move a stalled claim forward</h2>

<p>You cannot make an insurer decide on your schedule, but you can make it easier for the file to move and harder for it to sit. These steps work for most residential and commercial claims.</p>

<figure><img src="/assets/img/stock/claim-evidence-organized-desk.webp" alt="Adjuster organizing printed claim photos and notes on a desk" width="1536" height="1024" loading="lazy" decoding="async"><figcaption>An organized claim file with dated correspondence makes it easier to show where a claim has stalled.</figcaption></figure>

<ol>
<li><strong>Build a written timeline.</strong> List the date of loss, the date you reported it, each inspection, each request for information, each response you sent, and each payment. Our <a href="/claim-documentation-checklist/">claim documentation checklist</a> can help you gather the pieces.</li>
<li><strong>Move the conversation to writing.</strong> Phone calls are fine for scheduling, but follow every call with a short email that summarizes what was said and what happens next.</li>
<li><strong>Ask direct questions.</strong> "What information do you still need to make a decision?" and "By what date do you expect to accept or reject the claim?" are fair questions. Ask for written answers.</li>
<li><strong>Close every open request.</strong> If anything is still outstanding on your side, send it with a cover note listing exactly what is enclosed.</li>
<li><strong>Escalate inside the company.</strong> If the adjuster is unresponsive, ask for the supervisor or claims manager by name and send them your timeline.</li>
<li><strong>Protect the property.</strong> Keep making reasonable temporary repairs and saving receipts. A delay does not pause your duty to prevent further damage.</li>
<li><strong>Watch the policy deadlines.</strong> Many policies set time limits for proof of loss, completing repairs to recover depreciation, and filing suit. A slow claim does not stop those clocks.</li>
</ol>

<h2>Filing a complaint with the Texas Department of Insurance</h2>

<p>If written follow-up does not produce a decision, a complaint to the <a href="https://www.tdi.texas.gov/consumer/complfrm.html" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Texas Department of Insurance</a> is a reasonable next step. TDI forwards the complaint to the insurer and asks for a response. It does not set the value of your claim, order a specific payment, or act as your lawyer, but a complaint can prompt a closer look at a file that has been sitting.</p>

<p>Keep the complaint factual and short. Include your policy and claim numbers, your timeline, the specific deadlines you believe were missed, and copies of the key letters or emails. Avoid speculation about motives. Facts carry more weight.</p>

<h2>When to involve a public adjuster or an attorney</h2>

<p>A public adjuster represents the policyholder on the claim itself: documenting the loss, preparing a detailed estimate, answering the insurer's information requests, and handling day-to-day communication. Many owners bring in a public adjuster when a claim has stalled because the work of organizing and responding has become a second job. Our <a href="/services/delayed-insurance-claims/">delayed insurance claims</a> service is built around exactly that. If the problem is a low payment rather than a slow one, see our page on <a href="/services/underpaid-insurance-claims/">underpaid insurance claims</a>.</p>

<p>Some situations call for an attorney instead of, or in addition to, a public adjuster:</p>
<ul>
<li>The Prompt Payment deadlines appear to have passed with no written decision or explanation.</li>
<li>The insurer requests an examination under oath, or suggests misrepresentation or fraud.</li>
<li>Your claim was denied and you are considering a lawsuit. Texas Insurance Code <a href="https://statutes.capitol.texas.gov/Docs/IN/htm/IN.542A.htm" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Chapter 542A</a> requires written pre-suit notice at least 61 days before suing on many weather-related property claims.</li>
<li>A contractual deadline to file suit is approaching. Many Texas property policies use a limit of two years and one day from when the claim accrued, but your own policy controls.</li>
</ul>

<p>Public adjusters are licensed by TDI under Texas Insurance Code Chapter 4102 and are not attorneys. A good public adjuster will tell you plainly when a question has moved into legal territory.</p>

<h2>Getting insurance claim help in San Marcos</h2>

<p>A delayed claim is frustrating, but it is usually fixable with organization, clear written requests, and a firm grasp of the deadlines. If you would like a second set of eyes on where your claim stands, our San Marcos public adjuster team can review your file at no cost. Start with a <a href="/free-claim-review/">Free Claim Review</a>, or call [[phone]]. Fees are disclosed in a written contract before any work begins; Texas law caps public adjuster compensation at 10% of the insurance settlement.</p>
